CPython uses a thread-local variable for the current thread state where
the compiler has one, and falls back to the PyThread_tss_*() API where
it does not -- but it decides that by asking the COMPILER, and gcc
offers __thread on every target whether or not the C library can support
it.

On m68k a thread-local access compiles into a call to __m68k_read_tp,
which only a C library setting up an ELF TLS block per thread can
provide. SuckOS's threads carry their state in their descriptors, so the
link fails with hundreds of undefined references to that symbol.

This takes the fallback, which is what it is for.

diff -ru a/Include/pyport.h b/Include/pyport.h
--- a/Include/pyport.h	2026-09-22 15:50:10.811044972 -0600
+++ b/Include/pyport.h	2026-09-22 15:50:10.825192411 -0600
@@ -513,6 +513,18 @@
 #    ifdef HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL
 #      error "HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL is already defined"
 #    endif
+#    ifdef __mc68000__
+       /*
+        * m68k has no thread register, and a thread-local access
+        * therefore compiles into a call to __m68k_read_tp -- which a C
+        * library can only provide by setting up an ELF TLS block per
+        * thread. SuckOS's does not; its threads carry their state in
+        * their descriptors instead. CPython's own fallback, the
+        * PyThread_tss_*() API, is what is used here, and it is the
+        * reason that fallback exists.
+        */
+#      define Py_NO_THREAD_LOCAL 1
+#    endif
 #    define HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL 1
 #    ifdef thread_local
 #      define _Py_thread_local thread_local
@@ -526,6 +538,10 @@
        // fall back to the PyThread_tss_*() API, or ignore.
 #      undef HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL
 #    endif
+#    ifdef Py_NO_THREAD_LOCAL
+#      undef HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL
+#      undef _Py_thread_local
+#    endif
 #  endif
 #endif
 
